# Normalize line endings on commit. Repository content is uniformly LF (verified
# in the 2026-08-23 git-repo audit); this locks that in against any clone with a
# misconfigured core.autocrlf, instead of relying on every writer's local config.
# Working-tree checkout endings still follow each machine's own settings.
* text=auto

# ── Scripts, where the working-tree ending is CORRECTNESS, not preference ──
# The rule above deliberately leaves checkout endings to each machine, which is
# right for source files. It is wrong for these two, and both directions were
# genuinely broken on a stock Git-for-Windows clone (whose SYSTEM gitconfig ships
# core.autocrlf=true). Verified 2026-09-09 by checking the blobs out under that
# setting rather than reasoning about it:
#
#   *.sh  came out CRLF, making the shebang `#!/usr/bin/env bash` — the
#         `bad interpreter: ^M` failure. setup.sh and start.sh were both hit.
#   *.bat needs the opposite: cmd.exe is only reliably LF-tolerant for simple
#         one-command-per-line files, and start.bat carries a multi-line
#         `if (...) else (...)` block.
#
# Pinning both makes every script correct on any clone regardless of local
# config, instead of correct only where someone happened to set core.eol.
*.sh  text eol=lf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
